    "bounding box" : [ 150.8, 558.889, 239.081, 569.783 ],
    "heading level" : 1,
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 10.889,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "MALAY STATES"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 59,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 219.2, 546.733, 396.08, 552.764 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 6.028,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "The principal river of the state is the Pahang, from which it takes"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 2,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 20.2, 545.969, 204.131, 552.194 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 6.222,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "18th century a raja of the royal house of Menangkabu came from"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 60,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 213.0, 517.768, 396.994, 546.803 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 5.833,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "its name. At a distance of 180 m. from the coast this river is formed by two others named respectively the Jelai and the Tembeling. The former is joined 20 m. farther up stream by the Lipis, which has its rise in the mountains which form the boundary with Perak. The Jelai itself has its rise also in a more northerly portion of this"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 3,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 19.8, 534.476, 204.119, 546.112 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 5.833,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Sumatra to rule over the federation of small states, each of which continued to be governed in all its local affairs by its own chief and"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 4,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 20.0, 528.676, 204.066, 534.318 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 5.639,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "by the village and other councils sanctioned by ancient custom, The"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 5,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 20.2, 522.69, 203.505, 528.526 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 5.833,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Sumatran raja took the title of Iang-di-per-Tuan of Sri Menanti,"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 6,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 20.0, 517.076, 203.717, 522.718 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 5.639,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Although they bore the name of the Nine States,  only six seem"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 61,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 213.2, 511.968, 397.64, 517.999 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 6.028,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "range, while its two principal tributaries aboye the mouth of the"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 7,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 20.2, 499.783, 204.364, 517.112 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 5.833,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "to have belonged to the federation.during the time of which history speaks. These are Sri Menanti, Johol, Tampin, Rembau, Jelebu, and Sungei Ujong. Later the two latter separated themselves"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 62,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 213.4, 506.367, 395.953, 512.203 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 5.833,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Lipis, the Telom and the Serau, rise, the one in the plateau which"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 63,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 213.2, 500.475, 396.324, 506.506 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 6.028,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "divides Perak from Pahang, the other in the hills which separate"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 64,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 213.2, 460.075, 398.035, 500.511 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 5.833,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Pahang from Kelantan. The Tembeling has its rise in the hills which divide Pahang from Kelantan, but some of its tributaries rise on the Trengganu frontier, while the largest of its confluents comes from the hills in which the Kuantan River takes its rise. The Pahang is navigable for large boats as far as Kuala Lipis, 200 m. from the mouth, and light-draught launches can also get up to that point. Smaller boats can be taken some 80 m: higher up the"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 8,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 20.2, 493.983, 205.729, 499.625 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 5.639,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "from the confederation. Ancient tradition says that the names of"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 9,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 20.4, 488.183, 204.588, 494.019 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 5.833,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "the nine states were originally Klang, Jelebu, Sungei Ujong, Johol,"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 10,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 20.4, 482.383, 204.23, 488.025 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 5.639,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Segamat, Pasir Besar, Naning, Rembau and Jelai. Of these Klang"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 11,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 20.2, 464.997, 205.151, 482.526 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 5.833,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "was annexed by Selangor, Segamat and Pasir Besar by Johor, and Naning by Malacca. During the last years of the 18th century the Iang-di-per-Tuan appointed an Iang-di-per-Tuan Muda to rule"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 12,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 20.8, 459.183, 205.179, 465.214 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 6.028,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Rembau, and the state of Tampin was created to provide for the"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 65,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 213.4, 454.275, 397.458, 459.917 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 5.639,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Jelai and Telom. The river, however, as a waterway is of little"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 13,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 20.4, 453.49, 204.794, 459.132 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 5.639,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "family of the new chief. In 1887 the governor of the Straits Settle-"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 66,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 213.6, 448.475, 396.01, 453.922 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 5.444,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "use, since it is uniformly shallow. The Rompin and Kuantan"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 14,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 20.6, 447.69, 204.719, 453.526 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 5.833,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "ments sent Mr Martin Lister to the Negri Sembilan, which had"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 67,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 213.4, 442.676, 397.279, 448.512 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 5.833,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "rivers are somewhat more easily navigated for the first 30 m. of their"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 15,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 20.4, 441.89, 204.67, 447.337 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 5.444,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "become disintegrated, and by his influence the ancient federal"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 68,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 213.4, 436.876, 397.497, 442.907 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 6.028,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "course, but taken as a whole the waterways of Pahang are of little"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 16,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 20.6, 418.69, 205.673, 441.819 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 5.833,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "system was revived under the control of a Resident appointed by the governor. The states which formed this new confederation were Johol, Ulu Muar, Jempol, Terachi, Inas, Gunong Pasir, Rembau, Tampin and Gemencheh. Prior to this, in 1873, owing to a civil"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 69,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 213.6, 419.583, 396.679, 436.912 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 5.833,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "value. The interior of Pahang is chiefly noted for its auriferous deposits. Gunong Tahan is situated on the boundary between Pahang and Kelantan. Its height is estimated at 8000 ft. above"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 70,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 213.8, 413.676, 396.956, 419.123 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 5.444,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "sea-level, but it has never yet been ascended. Pahang, like the states"
  }, {
    "type" : "list",
    "pdfua_tag" : "L",
    "id" : 17,
    "level" : "1",
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 20.6, 361.218, 205.638, 418.726 ],
    "numbering style" : "english letters",
    "number of list items" : 2,
    "list items" : [ {
      "type" : "list item",
      "pdfua_tag" : "LI",
      "page number" : 1,
      "bounding box" : [ 20.6, 383.997, 205.638, 418.726 ],
      "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
      "font size" : 6.028,
      "text color" : "[0.0]",
      "content" : "war in Sungei Ujong, Sir Andrew Clarke sent a military force to that state, put an end to the disturbances, and placed the country under the control of a British Resident. Jelebu was taken under British protection in 1886, and was thenceforth managed by a magistrate under the orders of the Resident of Sungei Ujong. In 1896, when the federation of all the Malayan states under British control",
      "kids" : [ ]
    }, {
      "type" : "list item",
      "pdfua_tag" : "LI",
      "page number" : 1,
      "bounding box" : [ 20.8, 361.218, 205.232, 383.946 ],
      "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
      "font size" : 5.639,
      "text color" : "[0.0]",
      "content" : "was effected, Sungei Ujong and Jelebu were reunited to the confederation of small states from which they had so long been separated and the whole, under the old name of the Negri Sembilan, or Nine States, was placed under one Resident.",
      "kids" : [ ]
    } ]
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 71,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 213.8, 407.984, 397.37, 413.626 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 5.639,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "on the west coast, is covered almost entirely by one vast forest,"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 72,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 213.8, 396.477, 397.947, 408.02 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 5.833,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "but in the Lipis valley, which formerly was thickly populated, there is a considerable expanse of open grass plain unlike anything to be"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 73,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 213.8, 390.677, 398.121, 396.319 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 5.639,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "seen on the western sea-board. The coast is for the most part a"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 74,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 214.0, 379.117, 397.513, 390.713 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 5.833,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "sandy beach fringed with caswarina trees and there are only a few patches of mangrove-swamp throughout its entire length."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 75,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 220.2, 371.976, 398.718, 378.007 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 6.028,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "The ancient name of Pahang was Indrapura. It is mentioned in"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 76,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 214.0, 343.29, 398.741, 371.926 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 5.833,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "the history of Hang Tuah, the great Malacca brave, who flourished in the 16th century, and succeeded in abducting a daughter of the then ruling house of Pahang for his master, the sultan of Malacca. Prior to this, Pahang had been ruled by the Siamese. When Malacca fell into the hands of the Portuguese in"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 105,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 379.0, 358.096, 397.12, 362.959 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 4.861,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "fiistery."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 18,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 27.0, 355.417, 206.118, 361.448 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 6.028,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "The population of the Negri Sembilan, which according to the"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 19,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 20.8, 349.504, 205.505, 354.951 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 5.444,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "census taken in April 1891 was only 70,730, had increased to 96,028"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 20,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 20.8, 343.597, 205.616, 349.433 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 5.833,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "by 1901, and was estimated at 119,454 in 1905. Of these 46,500"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 21,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 20.8, 320.797, 207.028, 343.639 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 5.639,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "are Chinese, 65,000 Malays, 6700 Tamils, and goo Europeans and Eurasians. The births registered slightly exceed the deaths in number, there being a large Malay population in the Negri Sembilan among whom the proportion of women to men is fair, a condition of"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 77,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 214.4, 314.597, 398.182, 343.131 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 5.639,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "1511 the sultan, Muhammad Shah, fled to Pahang, and the present ruling house claims to have been descended from him. The title of the ruler of Pahang was Bendahara until 1882, when the present (1902) ruler, Wan Ahmad, assumed the title of sultan, taking the name of Sultan Ahmad Maatham Shah. Up to that time the Benda-"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 22,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 21.0, 309.565, 205.736, 321.028 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 6.028,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "things not found in localities where the inhabitants are mostly Chinese immigrants. :"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 78,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 214.4, 308.905, 397.519, 314.741 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 5.833,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "hara had been installed on his accession by the sultan of Riau, and"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 23,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 27.4, 303.597, 205.445, 309.822 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 6.222,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "The revenue of the Negri Sembilan amounted to only $223,435"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 79,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 214.4, 303.105, 397.115, 308.747 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 5.639,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "held his office by virtue of that chief s letter of authority. About"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 24,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 21.2, 286.363, 205.783, 303.332 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 5.639,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "in 1888. In 1898 it had increased to $701,334, in 1900 to $1,251,366, and in 1905 to $2,335,534. The revenue for 1905 was derived mainly as follows: customs $1,268,602, land"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 80,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 215.0, 297.398, 397.573, 303.234 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 5.833,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "1855 the father of the present sultan died at Pekan, and his son"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 81,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 214.8, 291.706, 397.531, 297.153 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 5.444,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Bendahara Korish, who succeeded him, drove Wan Ahmad from"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 56,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 21.2, 289.696, 38.669, 294.559 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 4.861,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Finance"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 82,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 214.4, 285.906, 397.249, 291.742 ],
    "font" : "GlyphLessFont",
    "font size" : 5.833,
